# run-level-readers.allow — the adjudication half of scripts/run-level-reader-census.sh
#
# One row per file that BOTH reaches a workflow-run feed (gh run list / gh run
# view / .workflow_runs[] / actions/runs) AND reads a conclusion off it.
#
#   <path> <run-level-reference-count> <VERDICT> <reason>
#
# The count is per FILE, never a line number: line-anchored pins slide on
# insertion and red naming files the diff never touched. Adding a reference to an
# adjudicated file changes the count and reds; moving code inside it does not.
#
# VERDICTS
#   RUN-LEVEL        answers a question from the workflow-RUN conclusion. This is
#                    the hazard: continue-on-error makes a red job roll up green,
#                    so the answer is a true green about the wrong object.
#   JOB-LEVEL        the run feed is only an index; the verdict is read from jobs
#                    or check-runs, which is the honest level.
#   KNOWS-THE-CLASS  reads the run conclusion AND explicitly models the rollup.
#   HARNESS          a .test.sh; its matches are fixtures for the script it tests.
#   PROSE            the only matches are message or error strings.
#   CENSUS           this census itself: its matches are its own scan patterns and
#                    its own selftest fixtures. Adjudicated rather than excluded -
#                    an exclusion is a blind spot written into the instrument.
#
# RUN-LEVEL rows are ENUMERATED, NOT FAILED. This is a census: it establishes who
# reads what and makes that impossible to leave un-updated. Changing any reader's
# behaviour is separate work with its own row.

.github/workflows/deploy.yml 9 RUN-LEVEL RE-ADJUDICATED 2026-09-19 (task-e3e40d2feb73d4f6) after #19365 / commit 0bc6a13f8, which moved this file's anchor and added the NINTH reference below. THE FIRST REFERENCE is the path-diff base anchor, `gh run list --workflow=deploy.yml --branch=main --status=success --limit=30 --json databaseId,headSha` (now line 299; it stood alone at line 68 with --limit=1 until #19365). IN ITS OLD FORM IT WAS THE HAZARD THIS ROW IS NAMED FOR and the sentence that stood here said so: it read the newest run whose RUN ROLLUP said success and took that head as the base, so a run that deployed nothing -- superseded-at-start, already-covered, or a leg red laundered up by continue-on-error -- still advanced the base and silently dropped that push's paths from the diff. #19365 DEMOTED IT TO AN INDEX: it now only FEEDS candidates to the ninth reference's per-run job descent, which is what actually decides the anchor. What survives of the old hazard is one-directional and safe: a run whose rollup reads `failure` while its control-plane or instance leg concluded success is EXCLUDED from the feed, so the loop walks to an OLDER anchor -- a WIDER diff, never a narrower one -- and `--status` is the only field projected out of this list, so there is no `.conclusion` in the payload for anything to launder. The SIXTH reference (task-0c3069135d1b4bfd) is the `convergence` job, and it is JOB-LEVEL by construction: it reads `actions/runs/<id>/jobs` for the control-plane and instance legs' `.started_at` and NOTHING else -- a CLOCK, never a conclusion -- to date the cutoff past which a merged commit is not yet owed a deploy. There is no verdict for a run rollup to launder. It cannot regress into a run-level read either: the value is consumed as `--owed-before <ISO>`, so a `.conclusion` substituted there is not a date and the check refuses with HARNESS-UNAVAILABLE rather than passing. Note also this file carries NO continue-on-error anywhere, stated in report-deploy-failure's own comment, so the laundering shape the class is named for has no producer here. THE SEVENTH ARRIVED WITH task-103b5cc5ec4a8ccd, which gave the `convergence` job a non-zero conclusion, and it reads STATUS AND NOTHING ELSE: `gh run list --status=<st> --json databaseId,headSha,status`, once per st in queued,in_progress, asking only whether some deploy run has not yet had its turn. `conclusion` is not in the --json projection, so there is no verdict in the payload for a rollup to launder, and the field the adjudicator switches on is `status` -- a `.conclusion` substituted there matches none of queued|in_progress|waiting|requested|pending and falls to the `not in flight` arm, which makes the gate STRICTER (more likely to red) rather than greener. The direction of every possible failure of this read is toward the finding, never away from it: an unreadable list is passed as `--in-flight-source unknown`, which withholds the exit code while STILL emitting converged=false so report-convergence-failure files the issue anyway THE EIGHTH ARRIVED WITH task-01f48337d83a0d15 (the superseded-deploy exit at START): `gh run list --workflow=deploy.yml --branch=main --limit 80 --json databaseId,headSha,status,createdAt`, piped into scripts/deploy-supersede-exit.sh decide. It reads STATUS AND NOTHING ELSE -- `conclusion` is not in the projection -- asking only whether a NEWER main-ancestry sha is already in_progress or queued, decided by `git merge-base --is-ancestor`, never by createdAt or by any verdict; an unreadable list is an empty list and the decider PROCEEDS (fail-open: a wrongly-skipped run strands a commit, a wrongly-proceeding one only waits on the box lock). No rollup conclusion is consulted, so there is nothing for continue-on-error to launder. THE NINTH ARRIVED WITH #19365 (commit 0bc6a13f8): `gh api repos/<repo>/actions/runs/<cand_id>/jobs?per_page=100 --jq '[.jobs[] | select(.name == "control-plane" or .name == "instance") | select(.conclusion == "success")] | length'` (line 288), run once per candidate the first reference yields. THE FIELD IT READS IS `.jobs[].conclusion`, for the two DEPLOY LEGS SELECTED BY NAME -- never a run rollup -- and the count of legs at `success` is the whole verdict. IT IS A JOB-LEVEL DESCENT AND IT IS THE REMEDY FOR THIS ROW'S OWN HAZARD, not a new instance of it: both legs are `if: needs.changes.outputs.<leg> == 'true'`, so a run that touched no box carries them SKIPPED, which is not `success`; a leg conclusion is the one fact a run that deployed nothing cannot fake. NOTHING HERE IS LAUNDERABLE BY continue-on-error IN EITHER DIRECTION: this file carries NO continue-on-error anywhere (stated in report-deploy-failure's own comment), and the laundering shape is by construction RUN green over JOB `failure` -- this read is ON THE JOB, so a laundered leg would be seen as the red it is and the candidate REJECTED. THE DIRECTION OF EVERY FAILURE OF THE READ IS THE SAME, toward a WIDER diff and a redundant deploy: a `gh` error, an unparseable answer, an unknown or null conclusion and a non-numeric `cand_legs` all fall to `|| echo 0` and the `[ "${cand_legs:-0}" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null` guard, counting as `did not deploy`, so the loop keeps walking back; if no candidate in the 30-run window proves a leg, `base` becomes the OLDEST scanned success (the widest range available) and `last_deployed` stays EMPTY, which DISARMS the already-covered early exit rather than letting an unproven sha read as `on the box`. A redundant deploy coalesces on the box's own lock; a narrowed diff strands a commit -- the read is built to err toward the redundant one. NOT RE-VERDICTED to KNOWS-THE-CLASS although the comment block at lines 240-275 now models the rollup explicitly: the first reference still filters on a run-level `--status=success`, RUN-LEVEL rows are enumerated and not failed, and keeping the stricter bucket overclaims nothing.
scripts/crown-reconcile.sh 10 KNOWS-THE-CLASS the deploy-record reconciler. RE-ADJUDICATED 2026-09-06 (task-d015fb5b13973ef8) at the measured count with a reason that describes the CURRENT script — the previous row still cited a `select(.conclusion == "success")` filter over .workflow_runs[] that no longer exists (the header at :51 records its removal: THE LEGS OWN THE POPULATION — a run DELIVERED iff its control-plane or instance JOB concluded success, read from actions/runs/<id>/jobs, whatever the run's rollup says). The run conclusion IS still read, once, as `concl: (.conclusion // "none")` on the run list — and only to LABEL delivering/non-delivering runs as CANCELLED_DELIVERING / CANCELLED_NONDELIVERING in the POPULATION line (#16403); it never decides delivery, so the rollup hazard is modelled, not trusted: a run whose legs succeeded is counted delivered even when its rollup reads cancelled. The other nine references never read a conclusion: page assembly and the oldest-created_at pager stop (two, #16403 — the reads that moved the count 8->10), the dead-trigger ANY-STATUS in-window count, the `.status != "completed"` in-flight set (WRITTEN-IN-FLIGHT: declines to judge, never passes), max/min `.workflow_runs[]?.id` for existence-vs-absence ordering, the in_progress head_sha lookup, and the created_at window filter. An unreadable page is a FLOOR stated in the POPULATION line, never a pass.
scripts/registration-sample.sh 1 RUN-LEVEL reads `.workflow_runs[] | [.path, .status, .conclusion]` (line 217) and answers per-workflow status from the run rollup, not from check-runs
scripts/pds-pull-proof.sh 4 RUN-LEVEL the PDS pull-proof climb. THE VERDICT IS EARNED BY THE FIRST REFERENCE ALONE; THE OTHER THREE NEVER REACH A RUN CONCLUSION, and this row says which is which rather than letting one word cover four unlike things. FIRST (pre-existing; the `last_deploy` assignment in the step-0a deployed-sha block -- cited BY SYMBOL because this row previously carried `line 569` and the true line is now 1141, exactly the slide this file's header forbids anchoring on): `gh run list --workflow deploy.yml --branch main --limit 1 --json headSha,conclusion,createdAt`, projected as headSha[0:9]/conclusion/createdAt and printed on the `last deploy run` info line. THE HAZARD IS REAL FOR IT AND THE ROW SAYS SO: that `.conclusion` is the RUN rollup, so a deploy run whose job concluded `failure` under continue-on-error is shown to an operator as `success` -- a true green about the wrong object -- and THE DIRECTION IS THE BAD ONE, it under-reports a failed deploy. WHAT KEEPS IT SAFE MEANWHILE: it is `info` output and nothing else. No `ok=0`, no exit code, no condition and no later step consumes `$last_deploy`; an unreadable list is swallowed by `|| true` and prints `none visible`, so this read can make the operator colour wrong but can never make the climb PASS. The honest upgrade path is a descent to actions/runs/<id>/jobs, which the THIRD reference already demonstrates in this very file. SECOND (pre-existing; the `gh_out=` assignment feeding gate_d_verdict in the full-export preconditions): `gh run list --workflow deploy.yml --branch main --status in_progress --limit 5 --json databaseId -q '.[].databaseId'`. It reads STATUS AND IDS AND NOTHING ELSE -- `conclusion` is NOT in the --json projection, so there is no verdict in the payload for a rollup to launder -- and gh's exit status is captured apart from its stdout precisely so an API error and a genuinely empty result cannot read alike (PDS-D98); a non-zero rc produces gate_d_verdict's `UNKNOWN (gh exited N ...)` with return 1, which sets ok=0 and REFUSES the export. FAILURE DIRECTION: strictly toward the finding -- an unreadable list aborts the climb, it never green-lights it. THIRD, AND NEW (task-dffb1525b243bd62, arriving with the cond_d job-graph thaw; the `d_jobs=` assignment inside the per-run while loop): `gh run view <id> --json jobs -q '.jobs[] | [.name, .status, (.conclusion // "")] | @tsv'`, once per in-flight run id yielded by the second reference. IT IS A JOBS DESCENT, WHICH IS THE HONEST LEVEL: the `.conclusion` in that projection is a per-JOB conclusion, never the run rollup, and the question asked of it is not pass/fail at all. deploy_run_instance_verdict asks whether the `instance` job -- the only one of deploy.yml's two deploy jobs that ships to the source box -- is `skipped`, because `skipped` is the sole conclusion meaning the box was not and will not be touched; `success`, `failure` and a still-running empty conclusion are ALL classified `instance` alike, so the exact substitution continue-on-error performs (failure -> success) changes NOTHING this read decides. FAILURE DIRECTION, EVERY WAY IT CAN FAIL, ALL TOWARD THE FINDING: a non-zero `gh run view` yields `unknown:` and gate_d_verdict returns 1 (ok=0, export refused); a run listing no `instance` job is `unknown:` and refused rather than read as control-plane-only, since GitHub materialises a job only once the run reaches it and an absence is not evidence; an unrecognised verdict string falls to gate_d_verdict's `*)` catch-all, also `unknown:`, also refused. The ONLY arm that returns OK requires an `instance` conclusion literally equal to `skipped` on EVERY live run. FOURTH, AND NEW (same change; the `d_pairs+=` arm guarded by `[ "$d_jrc" -ne 0 ]`): IT IS PROSE INSIDE A RUN-LEVEL FILE -- the literal message text "gh run view exited N, so this run's job graph was never read". It issues no call, reaches no feed and reads no field; it matches the census SRC_RE only because the sentence names the command whose failure it reports. It is the operator-facing half of the third reference's fail-closed arm, so the direction it describes is the third reference's: refusal.
scripts/main-gate-watch.sh 3 KNOWS-THE-CLASS reads repos/<r>/actions/runs only to ENUMERATE, then asserts at check-run level; its header states the class verbatim - "`gh run list` itself launders the red (a run whose Sobelow job concluded `failure` reads `success` at the RUN level)" TWENTY-THIRD REFERENCE / +1 (2026-09-20, task-0a44c3bc96baa8cc): the full-oid gate's REFUSAL PROSE — a `red`/`echo` line that names `actions/runs?head_sha=` while explaining why an abbreviated sha is refused. It is a string in an error message, not a read: no feed is fetched on that line and no `.conclusion` is projected. The gate exists because that endpoint matches the FULL 40-character oid only and answers HTTP 200 with an EMPTY list for a prefix, which every transport and shape guard passes — so the count moves and the verdict does not.
scripts/main-gate-watch.test.sh 3 HARNESS fixtures and a stub `gh` for main-gate-watch.sh (task-0a44c3bc96baa8cc, §17). THREE code-line references, all inside offline scaffolding, and NOT ONE of them reads a `.conclusion` to answer anything. FIRST: the `*"/actions/runs"*` arm of §17's stub `gh` — a URL ROUTER that serves a recorded payload when the `head_sha=` it was handed is the full 40-character oid and `{"total_count": 0, "workflow_runs": []}` otherwise, which is exactly what GitHub does and is the defect being reproduced. SECOND and THIRD: the two control calls that assert that stub really is asymmetric (full -> 3 runs, prefix -> 0) before any verdict leans on it; both project `.workflow_runs | length`, a COUNT, never a conclusion. The `conclusion` tokens that put this file in the census at all are FIXTURE FIELDS in recorded check-run and workflow-run payloads, written into the harness and fed back through --check-runs-file/--runs-file. The assertions that follow are on main-gate-watch.sh's own verdict vocabulary (WAITING/MISSING/exit code), which the row for that script adjudicates KNOWS-THE-CLASS. So the count moves and the verdict does not.
scripts/main-verdict-presence.sh 3 KNOWS-THE-CLASS the absent-verdict read on main's tip (task-6cd940f66125a079). It reads repos/<r>/actions/runs?head_sha=<tip> ONCE, and the question it asks of `.conclusion` is EXISTENCE, not pass/fail: a run is counted as having published a verdict when its conclusion is one of success|failure|neutral|timed_out|action_required, matched as a WHOLE TOKEN, and as having published NONE when it is cancelled or startup_failure. The rollup hazard the class is named for cannot mislead this read in the direction that matters: continue-on-error laundering moves a conclusion from `failure` to `success`, and BOTH are PRESENT here, so a laundered run is classified identically to the honest one. The read never answers whether main is green -- that is scripts/main-gate-watch.sh's subject, at check-run level, one row above. The second reference is the in-flight discriminator, which reads `.status` and nothing else to tell 'no verdict YET' from 'no verdict EVER'. The script renders no required context and blocks no merge; it is fenced off pull_request in .github/workflows/main-gate-watch.yml. TWENTY-THIRD REFERENCE / +1 (2026-09-20, task-0a44c3bc96baa8cc): the full-oid gate's REFUSAL PROSE — a `red`/`echo` line that names `actions/runs?head_sha=` while explaining why an abbreviated sha is refused. It is a string in an error message, not a read: no feed is fetched on that line and no `.conclusion` is projected. The gate exists because that endpoint matches the FULL 40-character oid only and answers HTTP 200 with an EMPTY list for a prefix, which every transport and shape guard passes — so the count moves and the verdict does not.
scripts/release-scan.sh 5 KNOWS-THE-CLASS reads .workflow_runs[].conclusion and models the rollup explicitly, printing "its check suite rolled up green, so GitHub excluded it: continue-on-error" as a first-class case
scripts/absent-context-census.sh 12 JOB-LEVEL reads .workflow_runs[] for {id, path, status, run_attempt, conclusion}, then descends to actions/runs/<id>/jobs for the verdict. CORRECTION 2026-09-17 (#18819, task-b746219d193c5c60): this row used to read "for {id, path, status, run_attempt} only - never .conclusion", and that clause became FALSE when the startup_failure class landed. The count did NOT move - the field was added to jq projections that already matched SRC_RE, and the new `conclusion=$(jq -r '.conclusion // ""')` read matches no SRC_RE pattern - so this census could never have caught the drift, which is exactly why the clause is corrected by hand here. THE VERDICT IS UNCHANGED AND THE REASON IS NARROW: .conclusion is read for ONE question, `is_startup_failure`, a WHOLE-TOKEN case match on the single value `startup_failure`. That value is not a verdict about whether anything passed - a startup_failure run is refused at creation and publishes ZERO jobs and ZERO check runs, so it is the one conclusion that means the job level has NOTHING to descend to. Reading it is how this script avoids answering from a run rollup about a run that has no rollup. Whether a required context is PRESENT is still read from the check-runs feed. SECOND CORRECTION 2026-09-24 (task-faadd040f770153f): the clause that stood here said every other conclusion, cancelled included, is ignored entirely - that is now FALSE for `cancelled`, and it is corrected by hand for the same reason as the first correction: the count did NOT move (12 before, 12 after - the new `is_cancelled` read is a case match on the already-projected $conclusion, and matches no SRC_RE pattern), so this census could never have caught it. RULING - STILL JOB-LEVEL, and the argument is the one that kept startup_failure JOB-LEVEL, extended by one value: .conclusion is now read for TWO whole-token questions, `startup_failure` and `cancelled`, and BOTH choose a REMEDY LINE, never a verdict. The census already knows the context is ABSENT (from check-runs) before it looks at the run; the conclusion only decides whether the printed remedy says "re-dispatch" (CANCELLED_CURRENT - the run was stopped before it rendered, the #16709 concurrency-eviction case) or "rebase, or edit the spec" (NAME_NOT_IN_RUN - it executed and carried no such job). Both classes exit 1 and name the context, so no conclusion value can turn a scream into a pass. continue-on-error laundering, the hazard RUN-LEVEL names, still cannot reach this read: laundering moves a run conclusion from failure to success, and those two values fall to the SAME arm (NAME_NOT_IN_RUN) - the script never branches between them. `cancelled` is not a value laundering produces. Eleventh (2026-09-03, #15855, task-82059e31bcccdbd7): read_jobs_started_count/1, a second actions/runs/<id>/jobs descent that counts job rows whose .status is in_progress or completed (whitelist, -1 UNREADABLE fail-closed) to tell a queued run whose jobs NEVER started (PHANTOM, no remedy) from one whose jobs were dispatched — a jobs-level read of STATUS, never .conclusion; the verdict stays JOB-LEVEL TWENTY-THIRD REFERENCE / +1 (2026-09-20, task-0a44c3bc96baa8cc): the full-oid gate's REFUSAL PROSE — a `red`/`echo` line that names `actions/runs?head_sha=` while explaining why an abbreviated sha is refused. It is a string in an error message, not a read: no feed is fetched on that line and no `.conclusion` is projected. The gate exists because that endpoint matches the FULL 40-character oid only and answers HTTP 200 with an EMPTY list for a prefix, which every transport and shape guard passes — so the count moves and the verdict does not.
scripts/absent-context-census.test.sh 34 HARNESS fixtures for absent-context-census.sh. The three new references arrived with the not-yet-created-job fix: the age guard (D1C, UNDISPATCHED_YOUNG), the needs:-gated pair (D4C DISPATCHED_PENDING / D4D NAME_NOT_IN_RUN), and the both-limbs queue fixture (D10) -- four fixtures added, one removed with the old D4B in_progress mutation, net +3. Every one is a `jq` REWRITE of a captured .workflow_runs[] payload fed back through --fixtures; the fields they set are .status, .created_at, .run_attempt and .id, and NOT ONE of them reads .conclusion. The assertions that follow are on absent-context-census.sh's own class and VERDICT output, which the row above adjudicates JOB-LEVEL. So the count moves and the verdict does not -- 2026-09-01, +5 with the orphan/dormancy split (section 5c): a proved orphan, the same tuple on a LIVE head, a DISPATCHED tuple, the liveness-unreadable fail-closed arm, and the dormant-PR flip. Re-read before the count was moved: every one is the SAME shape as the rows above -- a `jq` rewrite of the captured .workflow_runs[] payload fed back through --fixtures, setting .created_at, .updated_at and .head_sha, plus new jobs-<id>.json and main-head.json fixtures. MEASURED: zero of the five read .conclusion. The assertions that follow are on the script's own class and VERDICT output, which the row above adjudicates JOB-LEVEL. So the count moves and the verdict does not. Eighteenth (2026-09-03, #15855, task-82059e31bcccdbd7): fixture 5e for the never-started phantom shape (a queued run whose job rows never left the queue) — a fixture row, not a reader. Nineteenth (2026-09-08, #16831, task-70721d698d7b5666): the pagination fix's fake `gh` stub grew a URL-ROUTING arm — `*actions/runs\?head_sha=*|*actions/runs\?status=queued*|*/actions/runs)` — which serves a canned `{"total_count":0,"workflow_runs":[]}` so the walk under test can reach the end of a feed. MEASURED, not assumed: it is the ONLY line the SRC_RE gained (comm against the merge-base list: one added, zero removed), and it reads NO .conclusion — it is a path router in scaffolding, the same shape as every row above. So the count moves and the verdict does not. Twentieth through twenty-second (2026-09-17, #18819, task-b746219d193c5c60): section 2s, the startup_failure class, adds THREE `jq` rewrites of the captured .workflow_runs[] payload fed back through --fixtures. MEASURED, not assumed - `comm` against the merge-base list: three added, ZERO removed. They are the FIRST rows in this file to touch .conclusion, and the clause above about these fixtures never touching it is retired rather than quietly left standing: two SET .conclusion="startup_failure" and one SETS .conclusion="failure". They WRITE the field into a fixture; not one of them READS a run conclusion to answer anything. The `failure` fixture exists precisely to prove the whole-token match - a substring test for `failure` would steal that row into the startup class - and the assertions that follow are on absent-context-census.sh's own class name and its remedy prose, which the row above adjudicates JOB-LEVEL. So the count moves and the verdict does not. Twenty-third through twenty-seventh (#20042, commit 8d94b7a00, task-a0abaae6f64c0a9c) - ADJUDICATED LATE, 2026-09-24 (task-faadd040f770153f): that PR moved this file 22->27 without moving this row, so run-level-reader-census.sh was red on origin/main from its merge. MEASURED, not assumed - `comm` of the SRC_RE code lines at 8d94b7a00^ against 8d94b7a00: five added, ZERO removed, all in the re-arm sections (§3b, §9): three `jq` rewrites of .workflow_runs[] that SET .status (one also SETS .conclusion="startup_failure"), and two `jq -r` projections that read `.id` off .workflow_runs[] to address jobs fixtures. Not one READS a run conclusion. Twenty-eighth through thirty-fourth (2026-09-24, task-faadd040f770153f, the CANCELLED_CURRENT class): seven more `jq` rewrites of the captured .workflow_runs[] payload - §2c (six code lines: the cancelled-current arm, the success control, the two-line superseded pair, the rendered-context control, the cancelled re-run) and §9.4b (#16709 as measured, run 35890853900). MEASURED - `comm` against 8d94b7a00: seven added, zero removed. Every one SETS .conclusion (to cancelled or success) or .id/.created_at/.run_attempt; none READS a conclusion to answer anything. The assertions that follow are on absent-context-census.sh's own class name and remedy prose, which the row above adjudicates JOB-LEVEL. So the count moves and the verdict does not.
