scripts/crown-reconcile.test.sh 1 HARNESS fixtures for scripts/crown-reconcile.sh, which the KNOWS-THE-CLASS row above adjudicates. MEASURED, not assumed: the file holds exactly ONE SRC_RE line -- :594, `jq 'del(.workflow_runs[].updated_at)'`, section (c2d)'s no-span fixture, a REWRITE of a captured run page that strips `updated_at` so the refusal arm (ALIBI-INTERVAL-UNREADABLE) has something to refuse. It deletes a TIMESTAMP and reads no conclusion at all. Every other `conclusion` in this file is a fixture that SYNTHESIZES one for crown-reconcile.sh to consume, never a read that answers a question: :179 and :246-247 printf run rows hardcoding conclusion=success/status=completed; :209 and :250 append a deliberate in_progress + conclusion:null row so a harness that STOPPED filtering on status would show up as a moved count; :219-223 runs_add takes status and conclusion as PARAMETERS; :268 and :290 emit per-JOB leg conclusions, which is the level crown-reconcile.sh actually decides delivery at; :679-681 and :731 are a captured page and a cancelled-run rewrite; :1367 and :2089 are grep needles for the script's own prose. The assertions that follow are all on crown-reconcile.sh's POPULATION line and verdict output. So the harness reads no run rollup to answer anything, and the verdict the row above carries is the one that matters.
scripts/check-deploy-smoke.sh 1 PROSE the single match is an error string quoting deploy.yml's anchor
scripts/check-deployyml-filters.sh 3 PROSE RE-ADJUDICATED 2026-09-19 (task-e3e40d2feb73d4f6) after #19365 / commit 0bc6a13f8, which added the second and third matches. This row previously read `1 PROSE: the single match is an error string` and that sentence is no longer true of the file. ALL THREE MATCHES ARE STRING LITERALS THIS SCRIPT PRINTS OR PLANTS; NONE IS A READ -- the script never invokes `gh` at all, it parses .github/workflows/deploy.yml as YAML and as text, so there is no run feed, no conclusion and nothing for continue-on-error to launder, in any of the three. (1) LINE 169, PROSE: an error string in the unparseable-workflow arm, quoting deploy.yml's own anchor to say why that arm fails CLOSED -- an unparseable workflow gets a startup_failure run with total_jobs=0, report-deploy-failure never runs, and `the changes job's 'gh run list --status=success' anchor freezes`. It is emitted on the refusal path only. (2) LINE 860, PROSE: an error string in the anchor-selection case, naming the REMEDY the gate demands -- `select the newest run whose control-plane or instance JOB concluded success (gh api repos/<repo>/actions/runs/<id>/jobs)` -- printed only when deploy.yml FAILS that case. It describes the job-level descent now at deploy.yml:288; it does not perform one. (3) LINE 2381, FIXTURE LITERAL: a python string inside case 21's MUTATION, which reconstructs the PRE-#19365 run-level anchor (`base="$(gh run list --workflow=deploy.yml --branch=main --status=success --limit=1 --json headSha ...)"`) and splices it into a COPY of deploy.yml to prove this gate reds on exactly the hazard the deploy.yml row above narrates. It is an armed control, not a reader: the mutation refuses with `MUTATION ANCHORS matched %d/%d times` if its anchors are not unique and with `MUTATION produced an IDENTICAL file` if the splice changed nothing, so the literal cannot go silently inert. Every failure of this file is a refusal to certify deploy.yml, never a pass.
scripts/run-level-reader-census.sh 8 CENSUS its own SRC_RE/READ_RE patterns and the readers its --selftest plants; adjudicated, never excluded from its own walk
scripts/ci-measure.sh 21 JOB-LEVEL FOUR MORE ARRIVED 2026-09-20 (task-76e529e61d9e34d0) with the --per-job mode, and 17->21 is MEASURED by diffing this file's own SRC_RE against origin/main, not counted by eye. ONE of the four is a FEED READ and it is an INDEX, BY CONSTRUCTION: `gh api repos/<r>/actions/runs?event=pull_request&status=completed&per_page=100&page=N -q '.workflow_runs[] | [.id, .path, .created_at] | @tsv'`. `.conclusion` IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT FROM THAT PROJECTION — there is no run rollup in the payload for continue-on-error to launder, and a `.conclusion` substituted into any of the three fields is not an id, not a path and not a timestamp, so the walk would key rows on a verdict string and the per-workflow cap would collapse to one row per conclusion rather than silently answering the wrong question. THE SECOND is the jobs descent that carries every verdict this mode reads — `actions/runs/<id>/jobs`, one call per run id from the index — projected to {run_id, workflow_name, name, conclusion, started_at, completed_at, steps[]}; that `.conclusion` is a per-JOB conclusion, which is the honest level, and it is used ONLY to TALLY (cancelled / skipped / failure counts printed in their own columns), never to decide anything. THE OTHER TWO ARE PROSE: the header sentence 'from `actions/runs/<id>/jobs`, per JOB' and the report line '<N> job rows read from actions/runs/<id>/jobs' — neither issues a call nor reads a field. FAILURE DIRECTION, every way this mode can fail, is toward a REFUSAL and never toward a confident number: an empty run feed prints 'the pull_request run feed came back EMPTY … REFUSING to print a table over zero runs' and returns 1, and zero parsed job rows prints 'ZERO job rows parsed … REFUSING' and exits 1. It still answers nothing pass/fail — it is a cost table for the CI diet, and nothing gates on it. ORIGINAL ROW: the CI measurement harness (task-ee3fc32d1615bd58): reads repos/<r>/actions/runs only to ENUMERATE run ids for a window (lines 291-305), then descends to /runs/<id>/jobs and counts per-JOB conclusions (line 149, 308-309); it answers nothing pass/fail, it tallies job-minutes and reds for the CI-diet report. Lines 330-331 are fixture rows for its own selftest. Seventh reference (2026-09-03, #15797): enrich_prior's corrected prior-attempt read (arm v1d) — still per-job, still a tally. Eight more (2026-09-03, #15845, task-3f0fff73fdf57a2d): the --breaker mode enumerates PR runs per day and per breaker job through actions/runs and reads each Decide step's verdict off the job LOG (INHERITED-FROM-MAIN or not) to COUNT inherited reds before/after — a tally for the 09-06 report on task-e638b950726fea51, never a pass/fail answer; its selftest arms b1-b9 carry fixture rows of the same shape. SIXTEENTH AND SEVENTEENTH, 2026-09-09, #17108 25c73e08e (--job-latency), count 15->17 and the verdict RE-DERIVED by reading both, not by copying the census number. Both live in jl_fetch() and they are the same enumerate-then-descend shape as references one to four. The sixteenth lists the run feed -- `actions/workflows/<wf>/runs?event=<ev>&created=<lo>..<hi>` projected as `.workflow_runs[] | "\(.id) \(.created_at) \(.conclusion) \(.head_branch)"`. It is the ONE line in this file where a RUN `.conclusion` is even in the payload, and MEASURED: it is DISCARDED. The consumer is `while read -r rid rest`, and `rest` -- which holds created_at, conclusion and head_branch -- is never referenced again anywhere in the function; only `$rid` is. A run laundered from failure to success by continue-on-error therefore yields the identical id and the identical descent. The other projected field that IS consumed, `total_count`, is a COUNT of rows, not a verdict: it is compared against the number of rows actually listed so the mode shouts when GitHub's 1000-item list cap silently turns this census back into a sample. The seventeenth is that descent, `actions/runs/<id>/jobs?per_page=100`, projecting per-JOB `{name, run_id, conclusion, started_at, completed_at, steps:[...]}`; every latency and compute number the mode prints is computed by jl_analyze from those JOB and STEP rows. So the count moves and the verdict does not: JOB-LEVEL, and this mode is strictly further from the run rollup than the rows above it -- it is the only ci-measure reader that reaches a run conclusion at all and it throws it away.
scripts/main-red-breaker.sh 2 JOB-LEVEL the main-red circuit breaker (#15740): reads repos/<r>/actions/workflows/<wf>/runs?branch=main&status=completed only as an INDEX of candidate main run ids, then descends to /runs/<id>/jobs and compares per-STEP conclusions of the same-named job with this job's own step outcomes. TWO /runs/<id>/jobs reads since #16371 (M5, task-e65c78b1cd214237): one per walk-back candidate until a run is found in which the job actually EXECUTED, and the fallback read of the newest informative run when none did. Both are jobs reads; a run conclusion is only ever used to skip cancelled/skipped runs, never to answer. An unreadable main makes it LESS forgiving (exit 1). Count 1->2 adjudicated 2026-09-06 (task-ca0ae421efa0d919) after pr-meta reddened on main.

.github/workflows/elixir-main-red-attribution.yml 2 KNOWS-THE-CLASS reads `github.event.workflow_run.conclusion == 'failure'`, but ONLY as the trigger that decides whether to investigate at all -- it never answers a pass/fail question from the rollup. The verdict it publishes is computed from the failed steps' LOG (`gh run view --log-failed`, the second reference), which is job-level evidence by construction: a step that did not run contributes no ExUnit locator, and a run rollup cannot manufacture one. THE ROLLUP HAZARD IS INVERTED HERE, and that is why this is not RUN-LEVEL. The class's danger is a true green about the wrong object; this file's exposure is the opposite -- an upstream run laundered to `success` over a red job would leave this watcher SILENT, which loses an attribution rather than publishing a false one. Nothing merges on its output and no check-run it renders is required. The laundering shape also has no producer upstream: elixir.yml carries NO job-level `continue-on-error` anywhere (verified by scanning its job bodies, 2026-09-06), and its `elixir-gate` aggregator is fail-closed over an explicit allow-set of upstream results, so an elixir run that rolls up `success` with a failed job is not a shape that file can currently emit. The residual is stated in the workflow's own header rather than papered over: a CANCELLED elixir run (the pending-queue collapse elixir.yml's concurrency comment documents) is neither `failure` nor an attributable red, so this watcher is deliberately silent about it -- a collapsed run measured nothing, so it cannot have measured too little
scripts/rerun-transition-collect.sh 3 RUN-LEVEL the passive re-run collector (task-f89558a95762863a). IT IS RUN-LEVEL AND THE HAZARD IS REAL FOR IT, so the row says so rather than arguing its way to a softer class. The three references: `.workflow_runs[] | .conclusion` as latest_conclusion, `actions/runs/<id>/attempts/1` `.conclusion` as attempt1_conclusion, and the attempt-1 log read (which reads no conclusion at all — it greps the breaker's own accusing sentence). THE LAUNDERING APPLIES: a run whose attempt-2 rollup reads `success` may still hold a continue-on-error-masked failed job, so a row recorded as failure->success MAY be a rollup artefact rather than a real pass. THE DIRECTION IS THE BAD ONE and it is stated in the script header too: the hazard INFLATES the apparent flake count, which is precisely the number a future FLAKE-SUSPECTED verdict would be built on. WHAT KEEPS IT SAFE MEANWHILE: this file is a collector, not a gate. It renders no required context, no workflow runs it, nothing consumes the ledger, and it never exits non-zero on what it finds (0 = collected, 2 = could not read, 3 = config fault). No verdict about any pull request is computed from these conclusions today. The row is here so that when somebody DOES compute one, the census has already told them the numbers are run-level and need a jobs descent first — which is the honest upgrade path: read actions/runs/<id>/jobs per recorded row and re-derive attempt1/latest from the JOB conclusions, at which point this row becomes JOB-LEVEL.
scripts/main-workflow-rollup.sh 1 RUN-LEVEL the tree-rostered main health roll-up (task-…, run-level-reader-census follow-up). IT IS RUN-LEVEL AND THE HAZARD IS REAL FOR IT, so the row says so rather than arguing to a softer class. The single reference: read_runs_tsv projects `.workflow_runs[] | {conclusion: (.conclusion // "")}` (line 208) and the roll-up loop branches on `newest_conclusion = "failure"` (lines 287, 308) to label each workflow RED-on-main or not — a verdict read straight off the RUN rollup, with no descent to actions/runs/<id>/jobs and no check-run read. THE LAUNDERING APPLIES: a workflow whose job concluded `failure` under continue-on-error but whose run rolled up `success` is reported here as a passing (non-RED) row, a true green about the wrong object. THE DIRECTION IS THE BAD ONE — it under-reports reds — and it is not the object this instrument exists for: its subject is THE DENOMINATOR (the roster is the tree, `.github/workflows/*.yml`, so a workflow that drifted out of branch protection or never ran cannot hide), and the per-workflow red/green line is secondary operator colour. WHAT KEEPS IT SAFE MEANWHILE: it renders no required context and blocks no merge — it is an operator report wired only as a shell HARNESS (shell-harnesses.yml main-workflow-rollup job), nothing consumes its output as a gate, and an unreadable run feed exits 3 CONFIGURATION FAULT rather than printing a green roster it did not measure. The honest upgrade path when someone DOES gate on a per-workflow verdict is a jobs descent (read actions/runs/<id>/jobs and derive newest_conclusion from the JOB conclusions), at which point this row becomes JOB-LEVEL.
tooling/concept-map/ci-boundary.test.mjs 2 HARNESS fixtures for tooling/concept-map/ci-boundary.mjs's --from-ci verdict reader (task-3b79b6580fe877c6, #18298). MEASURED BY SYMBOL, not assumed: ci-boundary.mjs ITSELF matches SRC_RE ZERO times — it reads repos/<r>/commits/<sha>/check-runs and never a workflow-run feed, so the production reader is job-level BY CONSTRUCTION and correctly carries no row of its own. Both references in this harness sit on ONE captured CHECK-RUN fixture, REAL_RUN (:660-670), fed to the reader as payload({check_runs:[...]}): :664 html_url and :665 details_url are URL STRINGS that merely CONTAIN the substring actions/runs/<run id>/job/<job id>. NEITHER IS A READ. ci-boundary.mjs consumes them at :825 as `const url = run.html_url || run.details_url || null` and echoes the winner verbatim into the verdict line as the job URL — never parsed, never split, never compared. The file's `conclusion` occurrences are CHECK-RUN conclusions, which is the honest level and the object the reader actually decides on. FAILURE DIRECTION: the laundering hazard is IMMATERIAL here rather than merely avoided — a run rollup laundered from failure to success by continue-on-error cannot reach this code at all, because no workflow-run feed is read anywhere in either file; and the two matched strings are opaque to the reader, so success, failure and a still-running empty conclusion all classify alike through them. The honest upgrade path if ci-boundary.mjs ever descends from a RUN feed instead of check-runs: that change adds a row for ci-boundary.mjs and re-adjudicates this one.
scripts/scheduled-arm-health.sh 1 JOB-LEVEL the scheduled-arm reader (task-c1148783a9ee36e7, #18402). THE SINGLE REFERENCE IS THE JOB DESCENT ITSELF, not a run-level verdict: fetch_jobs() calls `actions/runs/<id>/jobs` — the exact upgrade path this census's RUN-LEVEL rows (main-workflow-rollup.sh, rerun-transition-collect.sh) name as what would make them JOB-LEVEL. The file matched SRC_RE for the first time BECAUSE that descent was added; before it, it read only `actions/workflows/<file>/runs` and was blind to the class. THE VERDICT IS COMPUTED FROM JOB CONCLUSIONS. The run-level `.conclusion` counts in summarise() are a POPULATION (how many scheduled runs succeeded at all), never the pass/fail answer: no row reaches `ok` without executed_job_names() comparing the job names the cron ran against the job names the push arm ran, and a scheduled success whose jobs were all skipped reds as ASSERTED NOTHING. THE LAUNDERING DIRECTION IS THE SAFE ONE EVEN WHERE IT APPLIES: a continue-on-error job laundering a run to `success` inflates the scheduled success count, which can only move a row from NEVER SUCCEEDED toward `ok` — and the job read is precisely the gate that then catches it, because that laundered run's jobs still carry the failure and the skips. WHAT KEEPS IT SAFE MEANWHILE: it renders no required context and blocks no merge (.github/required-checks.json's required set is exactly Elixir gate, Cloud gate, Console gate, PR references an active task); it is wired only as the schedule-only .github/workflows/scheduled-arm-health.yml; and an unreadable jobs payload exits 2 CANNOT MEASURE rather than falling through onto the `ok` path it was about to print.
tooling/gates/advisory-census.mjs 5 KNOWS-THE-CLASS the advisory check-run census (task-dee226be3107a98b, #18441). IT DOES READ RUN-LEVEL CONCLUSIONS AND THE ROLLUP HAZARD IS ITS SUBJECT, which is why the class is KNOWS and not JOB. Layer P's failure counts come from `/actions/workflows/<id>/runs?event=pull_request&status=failure` total_count — a RUN conclusion — and the file says so at its top in the layer contract: "It cannot tell you WHICH JOB inside a workflow failed", stated as a limit of the layer rather than papered over. The laundering is not merely acknowledged, it is COUNTED and ATTACHED: Layer S counts `continue-on-error: true` per workflow, 113 of 181 advisory names carry one, the classifier appends "AND its workflow carries continue-on-error, so a zero here is STRUCTURAL, not evidence of health" to every zero-failure verdict, and the run's single largest finding IS a laundered green (`reland-check`, 5499 PR runs, 0 failures, `continue-on-error: true` at JOB level, therefore structurally incapable of red). The reciprocal limit is stated in the same contract: a rerun overwrites a conclusion in place, so every failure count is a FLOOR and never an overcount. MEASURED, not assumed, on the five SRC_RE references (`//` is not stripped by the census, so three of the five are the prose above): line 24 the layer contract, 381 the endpoint warning, 427 the 1000-item pagination cap — all comments; and two CODE reads, neither of which decides anything about health — line 421 `/actions/runs?event=pull_request&created=>=<since>&per_page=1`, a repo-wide total used ONLY as the control band `sum(per-workflow) <= repo-wide` printed on every run, and line 538 `/actions/runs/<id>`, which reads `.path` and `.name` to map a check-run's details_url back to its workflow FILE and never touches `.conclusion`. The check-run level IS reached, in Layers H and M, where every rollup is paginated until `collected === total_count` and a short read is returned as `ok:false` and excluded, never counted. The script renders no check run, is wired into no workflow, and blocks no merge.
scripts/merge-check.sh 5 KNOWS-THE-CLASS RE-ADJUDICATED 2026-09-16 (task-7340fe78b609a436). This row read PROSE 1 while the script's only run-feed match was a printf remediation string. It is now a genuine READ: `mc_wf_main_census` fetches `actions/workflows/<file>/runs?branch=main` and reads `.conclusion` off each workflow_run, and `mc_name_to_workflow` fetches `actions/runs/<id>` for `.path` (a path read, not a verdict read). WHY THE RUN LEVEL IS THE RIGHT LEVEL HERE AND THE ROLLUP HAZARD IS MODELLED, NOT IGNORED: this read does not answer "did this PR pass" — the PR verdict is still read ONLY from `commits/<sha>/check-runs`, deduped newest-per-name, exactly as before (mc_rollup for the head, mc_main_census for the origin/main commit window). The workflow-history read answers a DIFFERENT and strictly narrower question, asked ONLY of names the commit-window census could not see at all (UNPROVEN): "when this path-filtered workflow last ran on main, did it fail?". THE LAUNDERING DIRECTION IS THE SAFE ONE AND IT IS LOAD-BEARING: a job-level `continue-on-error` makes a red job roll up as a green RUN, so this census can only UNDER-count main failures. Under-counting lowers k in `k/N`, which pushes the class toward OWN, which REFUSES. A laundered run can therefore cost a true permit; it can NEVER manufacture a false INHERITED, because a run reads `failure` only when a job that was NOT continue-on-error actually failed. Fail-closed is preserved by construction. An absence of main history stays UNPROVEN and still refuses. The count moves only if a sixth run-feed reference appears; a change that made this read answer the PR's own verdict would have to re-adjudicate to RUN-LEVEL.
scripts/main-red-predicate.sh 6 KNOWS-THE-CLASS the r19 main-red predicate, vendored by task-50373ad0a9edbaf4 (#18469), ADJUDICATED AGAINST THE PATCHED FILE (2026-09-16) AFTER AN EARLIER DRAFT OF THIS ROW CORRECTLY READ RUN-LEVEL ON THE PRE-PATCH VERSION -- the census refusal that produced it was substantive, not paperwork. IT READS THE RUN CONCLUSION AND THEN EXPLICITLY MODELS THE ROLLUP, which is this verdict verbatim rather than JOB-LEVEL: the run feed is NOT merely an index here, since `failure|timed_out|startup_failure` reds the workflow directly off the run. The rollup model is the descent at line 213, `repos/<r>/actions/runs/<id>/jobs --paginate`, projecting `[.jobs[]?|select(.conclusion=="failure")|.name]`: when the RUN says success and ANY job concluded failure, CONC is forced to failure and the row is annotated ` [RUN said success; FAILING JOB(S): <names>]`, so the laundering is both counted AND visible instead of being silently corrected. PROVED LIVE, not asserted: required-checks-drift.yml run 34968620058 reads RUN success with job `Required-check spec drift (advisory)` at failure, and the vendored file reds it with that annotation from a clean checkout. THE DESCENT CAN ACTUALLY FIRE -- line 189 requests `databaseId` in its `--json conclusion,headSha,createdAt,status,databaseId` projection, which an earlier draft of the patch omitted while still keying on it, a fix that would have printed `job descent NOT performed` for all 53 workflows forever; the vendored selftest arm `descent can fire` pins both halves. ONE RESIDUAL, MEASURED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS WITH A CONTROL AND RECORDED HERE RATHER THAN PAPERED OVER: the `no databaseId -- job descent NOT performed, this row is RUN-LEVEL ONLY` annotation at line 221 is emitted ONLY from the red arm at line 225, because the `success)` arm increments green and prints nothing. So if `databaseId` ever stops being delivered, a laundered success is counted GREEN with the warning discarded -- silent on exactly the class the annotation exists for. Measured with the file's own stub: NOID + run=success gives green 53, exit 0, annotation count 0, while the control NOID + run=failure emits it 53 times. That is latent today (the projection does request the field) and it is a verdict-path change, so it is reported to the tool's owner rather than altered by the vendoring PR. THE OTHER REFERENCES, named so the count of 4 is checkable: line 186 `gh run list --branch main --status completed --limit 200 --json name` projects ONLY `.name` and is the discrimination CONTROL that refuses when the feed shows fewer than 2 distinct workflow names, so no conclusion is in its payload to launder; line 247 `gh run list --workflow=<wf> --branch main --limit 50 --json conclusion,headSha,createdAt,status,databaseId` is the per-workflow verdict feed itself (the one the rollup model descends from); and line 60 is not a feed read at all -- it is the vendored selftest's positive assertion that greps THIS FILE for the databaseId projection and the runs/<id>/jobs call, the arm that reds if the descent is ever made unable to fire. COUNT RE-DERIVED 2026-09-16, 5->4, by gates/r20-main-collapse (task-9ee58a247b158826, PR #18556), BY READING THE PATCHED FILE RATHER THAN BY TRUSTING THE CENSUS NUMBER: that PR makes a CANCELLED newest main run a DESTROYED verdict instead of a verdict, and in doing so it DELETED a run-level reference -- the second API call `gh api repos/<r>/actions/workflows/<wf>/runs?branch=main&per_page=100 --jq '[.workflow_runs[]|select(.conclusion=="cancelled")]|length'` that used to count cancels for the unread annotation. Its replacement counts the same cancels with `jq` over `$WFEED`, the feed ALREADY fetched at line 247, so the cancel count is now derived from an existing read instead of a new one; the surviving `gh api .../runs?branch=main&per_page=1 --jq .total_count` is not a run-level reference (it reads a count, never a `.workflow_runs[]` conclusion, and the census patterns correctly do not match it). This is the census ratcheting in its BETTER direction -- one fewer reader, not a regression -- and the verdict stays KNOWS-THE-CLASS because the run-conclusion read at line 247 and the job descent at line 300 are both unchanged. COUNT RE-DERIVED 2026-09-17, 4->6, by gates-r21-w6 (task-0a48c7b64d5ab0f1, PR #18922), AGAIN BY READING THE PATCHED FILE: that PR adds a FRESHNESS CONTROL, and the census is RIGHT to notice it, because it turns a read the previous row explicitly adjudicated as NOT run-level into one that is. NEW REFERENCE 1 -- `gh api repos/<r>/actions/workflows/<wf>/runs?branch=main&per_page=1` is no longer projected with `--jq .total_count`; the whole response is fetched and reduced locally, so `.workflow_runs[0].created_at` and `.total_count` both come off one call instead of two. The payload now contains conclusions, so the census matches it -- correctly. IT READS A TIMESTAMP, NEVER A CONCLUSION: the only fields taken are `created_at` (the freshness comparison) and `total_count` (the unread annotation, previously its own call). NEW REFERENCE 2 -- the per-workflow verdict feed `gh run list --workflow=<wf> --branch main --limit 50 --json conclusion,headSha,createdAt,status,databaseId` is now issued TWICE on the stale path: once, and once more as a single re-read when the first page proves stale. It is the same call against the same object with the same projection, and the second page is fed into the same selector and the same job descent as the first, so it inherits the KNOWS-THE-CLASS treatment rather than adding a new class of reader. WHY THE CONTROL EXISTS, measured rather than argued: `gh run list --workflow=elixir.yml --branch main --limit 50` served a page whose newest row was 2026-08-23T16:53Z while the REST endpoint, queried seconds later, served 2026-09-17T08:37Z, and the same command repeated 3/3 immediately after returned today's rows. The predicate published that 25-day-old run as elixir.yml RED ON MAIN. In one live sweep at 08:50Z the control tripped on THREE workflows -- mobile.yml (13 days of lag), required-checks-drift.yml (12 days) and crown-reconcile.yml (4 hours) -- so the fault is systemic, not a one-off. ONE RETRY, NOT A LOOP, and that is deliberate: an instrument that keeps re-asking until it likes the answer is a worse failure than the one it is curing. THE RESIDUAL NAMED IN THE PREVIOUS ROW IS NOW CLOSED, and it was closed by the vendoring round rather than by this one: the `job descent NOT performed` annotation is emitted from the green arm too (as `UNDESCENDED-GREEN`, into the unread list where debt accrues, without changing the verdict), and the selftest arms `no-databaseId loud on GREENS` and its noise control `descended green stays silent` pin both directions. THE VERDICT STAYS KNOWS-THE-CLASS: the run-conclusion read and the job descent are both unchanged, and the two added references are a timestamp read and a repeat of an already-adjudicated call. Arms now 33/33, and main-red-owner.yml runs them in CI for the first time.
scripts/pr-required.sh 2 JOB-LEVEL the r19 required-four reader, vendored by task-50373ad0a9edbaf4 (#18469). THE RUN FEED IS NEVER A VERDICT HERE, AND NO `.conclusion` IS READ OFF A RUN ANYWHERE IN THE FILE. Every pass/fail answer comes from `repos/<r>/commits/<sha>/check-runs?per_page=100` (line 549) projected as `.check_runs[] | name/status/conclusion` -- the CHECK RUN, which is precisely the object continue-on-error does NOT launder, as this census's own header states. The verdict line is the awk at the foot of the file and it counts `$3=="success"` over those check-run rows. THE TWO MATCHES: line 538, `actions/runs?head_sha=<sha>&per_page=50` projected as `[.workflow_runs[]|select(.status!="completed")]|length` -- a LIVENESS COUNT of runs still moving, read from `.status` and never `.conclusion`, used for one purpose only, to split a required context with no check run into PENDING (something is still running, wait) versus ABSENT (nothing is running, re-fire). Substituting a `.conclusion` there matches none of the status values and the count falls to 0, which makes the tool say ABSENT rather than PENDING -- i.e. it hands the PR BACK rather than passing it, so every failure direction of this read is toward the finding and never toward a false green. Line 61 is not shipping code at all: it is the `*/actions/runs*) echo 0` arm of the network-free selftest's stub `gh`, the fixture that makes that very liveness count return zero. Neither reference can produce a pass/fail answer from a run rollup.
scripts/required4.sh 2 JOB-LEVEL the r19 presence-first required-four reader, vendored by task-50373ad0a9edbaf4 (#18469). THE VERDICT IS A CHECK-RUN READ AND THE RUN FEED IS ONLY AN INDEX. Both `VERDICT: n/4` and the exit code derive solely from `gh pr view --json statusCheckRollup` (line 32) -- statusCheckRollup rows are CHECK RUNS, the object continue-on-error cannot launder -- deduped newest-per-name and required to be PRESENT before they are allowed to be green. THE TWO MATCHES BOTH LIVE INSIDE probe_absent(), which exists to tell "queued behind a runner" from "never triggered" and is explicitly a JOB-LEVEL descent: line 180 `gh run list --workflow <wf>.yml --branch <br> --limit 30 --json databaseId,status,conclusion,headSha` filters to the PR head sha and is used to obtain a run DATABASE ID plus a human-readable line, and line 187 takes that id straight into `gh run view <id> --json jobs`, printing only jobs whose `.status!="completed"` -- the descent this class asks for, present by construction. The `.conclusion` at line 180 is emitted verbatim into a display string by jq and is never parsed or switched on; the function's own comment records WHY ("THIS FUNCTION PRINTS NO PARSED STATUS FIELD, DELIBERATELY"), after an earlier version split a joined string and printed success for a run that was QUEUED. Nothing in probe_absent can change `$G`, the count of green required check runs, so no run rollup reaches the verdict or the exit code.
scripts/ci-pr-checkrun-floor.sh 3 JOB-LEVEL the check-run floor measurer (task-dee226be3107a98b c2, #18498). NO `.conclusion` IS EVER READ OFF A RUN IN THIS FILE, and the verdict is a count of JOB ROWS, not of verdicts. FIRST REFERENCE (`actions/runs?head_sha=<sha>&per_page=100`): the run feed is an INDEX and nothing else -- the only fields consumed from it are `.id` (to descend) and `.path` (to name the workflow file, which is then matched against the four required-context workflows derived from .github/required-checks.json). The payload's `.conclusion` is never projected, parsed or switched on, so continue-on-error has nothing to launder into this read. SECOND REFERENCE (`actions/runs/<id>/jobs?per_page=100`): the descent this class asks for. It counts `len(jobs)` per run -- how many CHECK RUNS this workflow publishes on this head -- and separately counts jobs whose `.conclusion == "skipped"`, which is a COST question (a skipped job holds no runner and no queue slot), never a pass/fail question. The exact substitution continue-on-error performs, failure -> success, changes NEITHER count: a laundered job is still one job row and is still not `skipped`. FAILURE DIRECTION, BOTH READS, TOWARD THE FINDING: an unreadable run feed REFUSES with exit 4 and prints no number rather than reporting a smaller floor, and the discrimination control refuses outright when the feed returns 1 or 0 runs, because a feed that sees one thing cannot decompose a head. An unreadable jobs payload yields an empty job list for that run, which can only make the measured total SMALLER -- and the script's conclusion is that the floor is TOO HIGH to meet the target, so an undercount argues against its own verdict rather than for it. A SECOND REFUSAL WAS ADDED AFTER REBASING ONTO origin/main, and it is a READ of the SAME payload rather than a new reference, so this count stays 2: `incomplete_required_runs` re-reads the already-fetched run feed for `.status` (never `.conclusion`) and refuses with exit 4 when any required-context run has not concluded. It exists because the first filing of the floor claimed 26 with `zero variance` and a re-measurement read 22/24/26/26 -- every low reading was this script counting the job rows of a run still creating them, which is a PREFIX. That is a `.status` liveness read of exactly the shape already adjudicated JOB-LEVEL on scripts/pr-required.sh line 538, and its failure direction is toward the finding: it can only turn a number into a refusal, never a refusal into a number. The script renders no required context, blocks no merge, and is wired only as a paths-filtered tenant job in shell-harnesses.yml, where CI runs its hermetic `--selftest` (no network) and never the live measurement. TWENTY-THIRD REFERENCE / +1 (2026-09-20, task-0a44c3bc96baa8cc): the full-oid gate's REFUSAL PROSE — a `red`/`echo` line that names `actions/runs?head_sha=` while explaining why an abbreviated sha is refused. It is a string in an error message, not a read: no feed is fetched on that line and no `.conclusion` is projected. The gate exists because that endpoint matches the FULL 40-character oid only and answers HTTP 200 with an EMPTY list for a prefix, which every transport and shape guard passes — so the count moves and the verdict does not.
scripts/measurements/breaker-verdict-census-2026-09-20.sh 2 JOB-LEVEL the derivation behind breaker-before-after-2026-09-20.md (task-33682262f429d104), adjudicated 2026-09-21 by task-9749e764d87bb59d after it reddened `PR meta gates` unadjudicated on four consecutive main tips (b33ed64b1, 2ffd3dcbf, ab36b3bcd, 3ccfa1234). BOTH references reach the workflow-RUN feed `repos/$REPO/actions/workflows/$wf/runs?event=pull_request&created=...`, and NEITHER decides anything. THE FIRST (line 79) projects only `.total_count` — a POPULATION COUNT, not a verdict, and there is no `.conclusion` in that payload for a rollup to launder. THE SECOND (line 82) does project `.conclusion`, into column 5 of runs.tsv, and that column is where this file earns its adjudication rather than an exclusion: the value is bound as `concl` by the verdict loop's `while IFS=$'\t' read -r win wf run_id created concl` at line 103 and IS NEVER REFERENCED AGAIN. Verified by the token census `grep -n concl <file>` — `concl` appears at exactly two sites, the projection (82) and the binding (103) — against the CONTROL `jconcl`, the JOB conclusion, which appears at its binding (107) AND at its consumption (132), so the grep discriminates a carried value from a consumed one. What the run feed supplies is an INDEX: `.id` and `.created_at`, the latter sorted for the systematic sample at lines 95-101. Every verdict is read one level down — `repos/$REPO/actions/runs/$run_id/jobs` for the per-job conclusion, and `repos/$REPO/actions/jobs/$jid/logs` grepped for the five literal `main-red-breaker:` sentence openings — which is the honest level. The file is a dated one-shot measurement script and is run by no workflow; its own header states the population is EXACT from total_count while the verdict split is SAMPLED and labelled ESTIMATED. A regression into a true run-level read would change the reference count and red this row.
